  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA quiet morning across the area courtesy of broad high pressure\nacross the northeast CONUS, which is currently centered in upstate\nNew York. This has resulted in a clear and cool overnight via \nefficient radiational cooling. A large air / river water \ntemperature spread this morning and associated moisture flux has\nquickly helped to facilitate widespread river valley fog \nsoutheast of the Ohio River. Fog will be dense in some\nlocations, and will continue to monitor the need for a SPS for \nthe morning commute. Lows this morning will be seasonably cool, \nwith 50s across the lowlands, while 40s in the mountains. The \ntypical lowland valley cold spots could dip into the upper 40s, \nwith upper 30s not out of the question in the coldest of the \nnorthern mountain valleys.\n\nOnce fog dissipates shortly after sunrise, a partly cloudy and\ndry day is ahead with just a bit of fair weather Cu here and \nthere. The main story of the day will be significantly warmer\ntemperatures courtesy of return downsloping flow as high \npressure continues to shift northeast of the CWA. Highs today\nwill be ~ 10-12 degrees warmer than that of yesterday,\ntranslating to mid 80s to low 90s across the lowlands amid a\nnoted increase in humidity. Lows tonight will be ~ 10 degrees \nwarmer than this morning, with another round of river valley fog\nexpected. \n\nWednesday and Thursday will be the warmest days of the week,\nwith highs progged in the upper 80s to low 90s across much of\nthe lowlands. Southwest to west low-level flow will usher in an\nincreasingly humid Gulf airmass into the area around the\nperiphery of an upper ridge centered near the Tennessee Valley.\nThe combination of heat and humidity will result in heat \nindices Wednesday/Thursday afternoons reaching the mid 90s to \n100 across the lowlands. While much of the area remains dry on \nWednesday, some modest diurnal buoyancy could result in isolated\nshower/storm chances, primarily across our northern zones with \nthe approach of a cold front, although differential heating / \norographic effects could result in an isolated shower/storm \nfurther south.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe aforementioned frontal boundary is progged to become quasi-\nstationary (east-west) across the general Ohio Valley region \nWednesday night into the weekend, meandering north/south at\ntimes. This will result in a return of an unsettled pattern \nwith daily chances for showers/storms, generally favored in the \nafternoon/evening hours or when weak upper waves traverse \noverhead across the northern periphery of the upper ridge. \nConfidence in specific details continues to remain on the lower \nside at this time, with shower/storm chances being highly \ncorrelated to the position of the frontal boundary. \n\nGiven recent rainfall, and the return of a very moist airmass \nwith progged PWATs pushing 1.8-2.1\" at times, the concern for \nflash flooding will return, especially given at least some \npotential for west-east training showers/storms along the front\ngiven progged upper flow aligning with the surface front. WPC \nhas introduced a Marginal Risk for excessive rainfall across \nwestern portions of the CWA for late Wednesday night, while \nmuch of the area for Thursday into Thursday night. At present, \nrain chances are highest Thursday and Friday, then lower on \nSaturday as the front slides south of the region. Higher chances\nfor showers/storms then return late Sunday and Monday. \n\nThe threat for organized severe weather continues to be on the \nlower side given favorable kinematic fields generally being \ndisplaced too far to the north. This translates into weak shear \nand modest buoyancy amid rather saturated profiles, which could \nresult in isolated (diurnally driven) stronger storms Thursday \nonward, primary concern being damaging winds via wet microbursts.\n\nHigh temperatures Friday into the weekend will continue to be \nabove normal, albeit slightly cooler than Wednesday and Thursday.",
